DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmccrroopp  reads  a PBM, PGM, or PPM image as input, removes borders that
       are the background color, and produces the same type of image  as  out‐
       put.

       If you don't specify otherwise, ppnnmmccrroopp assumes the background color is
       whatever color the top left and right corners of the image are  and  if
       they  are  different  colors,  something  midway between them.  You can
       specify that the background is white  or  black  with  the  --wwhhiittee  and
       --bbllaacckk  options  or make ppnnmmccrroopp base its guess on all four corners in‐
       stead of just two with --ssiiddeess.

       By default, ppnnmmccrroopp chops off any stripe of background color it  finds,
       on  all  four sides.  You can tell ppnnmmccrroopp to remove only specific bor‐
       ders with the --lleefftt, --rriigghhtt,
         --ttoopp, and --bboottttoomm options.

       But note that ppnnmmccrroopp's determination of the background color is  inde‐
       pendent of which edges you crop, which may not be intuitive.  For exam‐
       ple, imagine an image with a blue border at the top and a black  border
       at  the  bottom and you say to crop the bottom (--bboottttoomm).  You may have
       expected to crop the black border, but you  actually  won't  crop  any‐
       thing,  because  ppnnmmccrroopp  considers the background color to be whatever
       color the top two corners are, which is blue, and there is no  blue  at
       the bottom of the image.  If you do want ppnnmmccrroopp to take the background
       color from the edges being cropped, use --bbgg--ccoorrnneerr.


       If you want to leave some border, use the --mmaarrggiinn option.  It will  not
       only  spare  some  of  the border from cropping, but will fill in (with
       what ppnnmmccrroopp considers the background color) if necessary to get up  to
       that size.

       If  the input is a multi-image stream, ppnnmmccrroopp processes each one inde‐
       pendently and produces a multi-image  stream  as  output.   It  chooses
       where  to  crop  independently  for each image.  So if you start with a
       stream of images of the same dimensions, you may end up with images  of
       differing dimensions.  Before Netpbm 10.37 (December 2006), ppnnmmccrroopp ig‐
       nored all input images but the first.

       If you want to chop a specific amount off the side  of  an  image,  use
       ppaammccuutt.

       If  you want to add different borders after removing the existing ones,
       use ppnnmmccaatt or ppaammccoommpp.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ), ppnnmmccrroopp recognizes the
       following command line options:



       --wwhhiittee Take white to be the background color.  ppnnmmccrroopp removes  borders
              which are white.

              You  may  specify  at  most  one of --bbllaacckk, --wwhhiittee, --ssiiddeess, --bbgg--
              ccoolloorr, and --bbgg--ccoorrnneerr.


       --bbllaacckk Take black to be the background color.  ppnnmmccrroopp  removes borders
              which are black.

              You  may  specify  at  most  one of --bbllaacckk, --wwhhiittee, --ssiiddeess, --bbgg--
              ccoolloorr, and --bbgg--ccoorrnneerr.


       --bbgg--ccoolloorr==_c_o_l_o_r
              This tells ppnnmmccrroopp what color is the background - it  will  crop
              areas of this color.  _c_o_l_o_r is a value that would be used as the
              argument    of    the    ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(())    library     routine
              ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .

              You  may  specify  at  most  one of --bbllaacckk, --wwhhiittee, --ssiiddeess, --bbgg--
              ccoolloorr, and --bbgg--ccoorrnneerr.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.86 (March 2019).


       --ssiiddeess Determine the background color from the colors of the four  cor‐
              ners  of  the input image.  ppnnmmccrroopp removes borders which are of
              the background color.

              If at least three of the four corners are the same  color,  ppnnmm‐‐
              ccrroopp  takes that as the background color.  If not, ppnnmmccrroopp looks
              for two corners of the same color in the following order, taking
              the  first found as the background color: top, left, right, bot‐
              tom.  If all four corners are different colors, ppnnmmccrroopp  assumes
              an average of the four colors as the background color.

              The --ssiiddeess option slows ppnnmmccrroopp down, as it reads the entire im‐
              age to determine the background color in addition to the  up  to
              three times that it would read it without --ssiiddeess.

              You  may  specify  at  most  one of --bbllaacckk, --wwhhiittee, --ssiiddeess, --bbgg--
              ccoolloorr, and --bbgg--ccoorrnneerr.


       --bbgg--ccoorrnneerr=={ttoopplleefftt|ttoopprriigghhtt|bboottttoommlleefftt|bboottttoommrriigghhtt
              This option indicates a corner  which  is  background.   ppnnmmccrroopp
              will  use  the  color of this corner as the background color and
              crop edges of that color.

              You may specify at most one  of  --bbllaacckk,  --wwhhiittee,  --ssiiddeess,  --bbgg--
              ccoolloorr, and --bbgg--ccoorrnneerr.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.86 (March 2019).


       --lleefftt  Remove any left border.


       --rriigghhtt Remove any right border.


       --ttoopp   Remove any top border.


       --bboottttoomm
              Remove any bottom border.


       --mmaarrggiinn==_p_i_x_e_l_s
              Leave  _p_i_x_e_l_s  pixels of border.  Expand the border to this size
              if necessary.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.29 (August 2005).


       --cclloosseenneessss==_c_l_o_s_e_n_e_s_s___p_e_r_c_e_n_t
              Any color in the image that is at least this close to the opera‐
              tive background color is considered to be background.

              You  can use this if the image has borders that vary slightly in
              color, such as would be the case in a  photograph.   Consider  a
              photograph  against  a  white  screen.   The color of the screen
              varies slightly with shading and dirt and  such,  but  is  still
              quite distinct in color from the subject of the photograph.  ppnn‐‐
              mmccrroopp will choose some particular shade as the background  color
              and if you specify an appropriate --cclloosseenneessss value, it will cor‐
              rectly identify all of the screen as background and crop it off.

              To implement more complex rules for identifying background,  use
              --bboorrddeerrffiillee.

              The  default  is  zero, which means a pixel's color must exactly
              match the background color for the pixel to be  considered  part
              of a background border.

              This  option  was  new in Netpbm 10.78 (March 2017).  With older
              Netpbm, colors must match exactly.


       --bboorrddeerrffiillee==_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
              Use the image in the file named _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e instead  of  the  input
              image  to determine where the borders of the input image are and
              the background color.

              Without this option, ppnnmmccrroopp examines the input image  and  fig‐
              ures out what part of the image is border and what part is fore‐
              ground (not border), as well as the background color.  With this
              option,  ppnnmmccrroopp  finds  the borders in one image, then uses the
              those four border sizes (left, right, top, bottom) in cropping a
              different  image.   Furthermore,  if you use --mmaarrggiinn to add bor‐
              ders, the color of those borders is the background color ppnnmmccrroopp
              detects in the border file.

              The  point  of this is that you may want to help ppnnmmccrroopp to come
              to a different conclusion as to where the borders are  and  what
              the  background  color is by preprocessing the input image.  For
              example, consider an image that has speckles  of  noise  in  its
              borders.   ppnnmmccrroopp  isn't  smart  enough  to  recognize these as
              noise; it sees them as foreground image.  So  ppnnmmccrroopp  considers
              most of your borders to be foreground and does not crop them off
              as you want.  To fix this, run the image  through  a  despeckler
              such  as ppbbmmcclleeaann and tell ppnnmmccrroopp to use the despeckled version
              of the image as the --bboorrddeerrffiillee image, but the original speckled
              version as the input image.  That way, you crop the borders, but
              retain the true foreground image, speckles and all.

              The border file must have the same number of images in it as the
              input  file;  the  background color determination for image N of
              the input is based on the image N of the border file.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.29 (August 2005).

              Before Netpbm 10.46 (March 2009), the original image and not the
              border  file  determines the background color.  ppnnmmccrroopp fails if
              there is no apparent background  color  in  the  original  image
              (i.e. the corners of the image don't have a common color).


       --bbllaannkk--iimmaaggee=={aabboorrtt|ppaassss|mmiinniimmiizzee|mmaaxxccrroopp}
              This determines how ppnnmmccrroopp handles an image which is entirely
                background  (blank),  a  case where cropping doesn't make much
              sense.



       abort

              program fails, with explanatory message (default)


       pass

              Output image is the same as the input image.
                    --mmaarrggiinn has no effect.


       minimize

              output is a single row, column,  or  pixel  (of  the  background
              color).
                    If  you  crop  both  vertically  and horizontally (the de‐
              fault), it is a
                    single pixel.  If you crop only vertically, a single  row,
              of the
                    original  width.   If  you crop only horizontally, it is a
              single column,
                    of the original height.

              This is a somewhat incongruous result; the  mathematically  con‐
              sistent
                      result  of cropping the background from an image that is
              entirely
                      background would be an image with no pixels at all.  But
              such a thing
                      does  not  exist in the Netpbm formats (and you probably
              wouldn't want
                      it anyway, because whoever processes this output may not
              tolerate
                      that).

              The background can be more than one color when you specify
                    --cclloosseenneessss,  so it matters which row, column, or pixel re‐
              mains.
                    If you crop on the top and not bottom, it is the last  row
              that remains.
                    If  you  crop on both the top and bottom, it is the middle
              row that
                    remains.  The other cases follow similarly.

              If you specify a margin (--mmaarrggiinn), the output image consists
                      entirely of the margins; there is no single row, column,
              or pixel
                      between the margins.  So with --mmaarrggiinn, the incongruity
                      mentioned above does not exist.  But before Netpbm 10.92
              (September
                      2020), --mmaarrggiinn was ignored with --bbllaannkk--iimmaaggee==mmiinniimmiizzee.


